  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 112 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nWednesday through Monday:\n\nShortwave phases with a stream of Gulf moisture as it \npropagates into Illinois on Wednesday, deepening a surface low \nand bringing more widespread rain chances to southern Wisconsin \n(30 to 50 percent chances) Temperatures in the upper 40s to low\n50s will keep precipitation as rain. As the low continues to \ndeepen and propagate eastward into Wednesday night, rain will \ngradually taper off from west to east. In addition, \nnorthwesterly winds are expected to increase and become \nsignificantly gusty. By Thursday morning, gusts around 30 mph \nare expected to be common. Near Lake Michigan, gusts up to 40 \nmph are possible. \n\nLow pressure deepens even more as it approaches the Atlantic, \nkeeping significant northerly wind gusts through Thursday and \nThursday night. With these northerly winds, expecting a strong \nband of lake effect showers to develop in Lake Michigan. With \nwater temperatures remaining in the 50s all the way to the \nnorthern tip of the lake, expecting this precipitation to remain\nrain, perhaps a mix of rain and snow as colder air moves in \naloft. This band will likely remain primarily in the open waters\ndue to dominantly northerly flow. \n\nHowever, periods of convergence and other mesoscale features \nmay allow the band to wobble onshore on and off Thursday through\nFriday. With these wobbles, still expecting a mix of rain and \nsnow in Lake Michigan counties. If this band were to make it \nonshore during the late overnight hours Thursday, temperatures \ninland are expected to be in the low 30s, allowing for brief \nperiods of accumulating snow potential. Confidence in snowfall \ndoes still remain quite low due to winds beginning to shift \nnorthwesterly by Thursday night, which would keep the band \noffshore. Keep an eye on the forecast for changes as we move \ntoward the event.\n\nFriday, temperatures recover into the upper 30s and low 40s, \nbringing an end to any snow potential, with lakeshore areas only\nseeing rain showers if the mesoband makes it onshore. Winds \nshift to northwesterly and gradually diminish through the day as\nlow pressure moves into the Atlantic Ocean and propagates \nnorthward. As winds continue to weaken overnight, the remaining \nlake effect band will no longer have any ability to impact \nsoutheastern Wisconsin.\n\nHigh pressure returns for the weekend, with light winds, highs \nin the upper 40s to low 50s, and lows in the upper 20s to low \n30s.\n\nAnother developing system in the central Plains will allow \nsoutherly winds to return Monday, bringing increasing \ntemperatures and cloud cover to start the week.\n\nMH",
